  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe severe weather threat this afternoon has quickly diminished\nacross most of the forecast area, as convective overturning has\nstabilized the airmass over northern Ohio. Instability gradient\nhas shifted south of the forecast area this afternoon, and \nweakening convection across northeast Indiana into northwest \nOhio may further reinforce the outflow boundary southward.\n\nCAMs have struggled to assimilate ongoing convection/precip \nover the past 12 hours, but the focus for potential severe \nthunderstorms and heavy rainfall is with any development \nupstream/over the area in the 00Z-03Z time frame. However, \nconfidence is decreasing on the magnitude of severe weather with\ninstability shunted south and west of the area and limited time\nfor recovery/advection into the local area. Storms will be \nmostly elevated during this timeframe, with some hail and heavy\nrainfall possible. If storms develop rooted in the boundary \nlayer, there could be a damaging wind risk and a brief window \nfor a tornado, although confidence is low and decreasing at the \nmoment. Heavy rainfall would also be a threat with additional \ndevelopment, with isolated flash flooding possible in areas with\ntraining storms and/or areas with saturated soils due to recent\nheavy rainfall.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe pesky frontal boundary that has been focused over the area\nmost of this week will finally push south of the area late\nSaturday as high pressure builds into the region from the north\nbehind a southeast moving shortwave from the lower Great Lakes\nto the mid Atlantic. As the front departs, a few\nisolated/scattered showers and storms may impact the area through\nmidday Saturday before dry conditions persist under the high\nthrough at least Tuesday. Seasonable temperatures expected\nthrough Monday before a brief warmup into midweek. A weak cold\nfront should move southeast through the area sometime mid to\nlate week before additional high pressure returns to the area by\nthe end of the week.",
